d = \frac{|(\mathbf{r_0}_2 - \mathbf{r_0}_1) \cdot (\mathbf{d_1} \times \mathbf{d_2})|}{|\mathbf{d_1} \times \mathbf{d_2}|}
\] where \(\mathbf{r_0}_1\) and \(\mathbf{r_0}_2\) are points on the first and second lines, respectively, and \(\mathbf{d_1}\) and \(\mathbf{d_2}\) are the direction vectors of the first and second lines, respectively. For the given lines, \(\mathbf{r_0}_1 = \begin{pmatrix} 3 \\ -1 \\ 2 \end{pmatrix}\), \(\mathbf{r_0}_2 = \begin{pmatrix} 0 \\ 0 \\ 4 \end{pmatrix}\), \(\mathbf{d_1} = \begin{pmatrix} 2 \\ -2 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix}\), and \(\mathbf{d_2} = \begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ 2 \\ -1 \end{pmatrix}\). The cross product \(\mathbf{d_1} \times \mathbf{d_2}\) is calculated as: \[
\mathbf{d_1} \times \mathbf{d_2} = \begin{vmatrix} \mathbf{i} & \mathbf{j} & \mathbf{k} \\ 2 & -2 & 1 \\ 1 & 2 & -1 \end{vmatrix} = \mathbf{i}(2 \cdot -1 - 1 \cdot 2) - \mathbf{j}(2 \cdot -1 - 1 \cdot 1) + \mathbf{k}(2 \cdot 2 - 1 \cdot -2) = \mathbf{i}(-2 - 2) - \mathbf{j}(-2 - 1) + \mathbf{k}(4 + 2) = \begin{pmatrix} -4 \\ 3 \\ 6 \end{pmatrix}
